<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christopher Wren</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1632">1632</a>–<a href="/years/1723">1723</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Sir Christopher Wren</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Christopher Wren was an English architect, astronomer, mathematician and physicist, born at East Knoyle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a> in 1632 and known for his work in the English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style. After the Great Fire of 1666 he was given responsibility for rebuilding 52 churches in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">City of London</a>, among them St Paul's Cathedral, completed in 1710, though the main creative work on several is now often attributed to others in his office, notably Nicholas Hawksmoor. He also designed the Royal Hospital Chelsea, the Old Royal Naval College at Greenwich and the south front of Hampton Court Palace. A founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a>, he was its president from 1680 to 1682.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christopher-wren.jpg" alt="Christopher Wren" width="330" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Godfrey Kneller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christopher_Wren_by_Godfrey_Kneller_1711.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The palace and its grounds had great influence on architecture and horticulture from the mid-17th century to the end of the 18th, seen in <b>Christopher Wren</b>'s work at Hampton Court Palace, Berlin Palace, the Palace of La Granja, Stockholm Palace and Esterházy Palace, among others.</blockquote><a href="/work/palace-of-versailles">Palace of Versailles · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The Principia grew out of a conversation in January 1684, when Edmond Halley, <b>Christopher Wren</b> and Robert Hooke discussed planetary motion.</blockquote><a href="/work/principia">Principia · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Christopher Wren</b>'s dome at St Paul's Cathedral in London, Karlskirche in Vienna, St. Nicholas Church in Prague and the Pantheon in Paris all pay homage to it.</blockquote><a href="/work/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The choir of <b>Christopher Wren</b>'s new St Paul's opened for worship with a thanksgiving service for the Peace of Ryswick.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-2">2 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
